<h2>7. Backlog Recovery Strategy</h2>
<p>Class 11 content is the silent killer of drop-year prep. Here's how to handle it without creating a separate backlog:</p>
<h3>Integration Method</h3>
<ol>
<li><strong>While studying Class 12 Mechanics</strong>, pre-study Class 11 prerequisites (Kinematics, Laws of Motion) as a 30-minute pre-reading block before each session.</li>
<li><strong>While studying Electrostatics</strong>, spend Week 1 reviewing Gravitation (similar math, same field concept).</li>
<li><strong>While studying Chemical Bonding in Class 12</strong>, go back to Periodic Classification in Class 11—same chapter sequence.</li>
</ol>
<h3>The 80-20 Fix</h3>
<ul>
<li>Identify the <strong>20% of Class 11 topics that appear in 80% of questions</strong>:
<ul>
<li><strong>Physics:</strong> Vectors, Kinematics, Newton's Laws, Work-Energy, Rotational basics</li>
<li><strong>Chemistry:</strong> Atomic Structure, Chemical Bonding, Mole Concept, Redox</li>
<li><strong>Maths:</strong> Quadratics, Trigonometry basics, Basic Probability, Binomial foundation</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
<h3>Daily Backlog Protocol</h3>
<ul>
<li>Dedicate <strong>30 minutes daily</strong> (use the 6:00-6:30 slot) exclusively to Class 11 revision.</li>
<li>Use <strong>Cengage Series Class 11</strong> for rapid problem practice in weak areas.</li>
<li>Mark any Class 11 concept that takes &gt;45 minutes to understand—schedule a coach/teacher doubt session immediately.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Source:</strong> JEE Advanced syllabus (jeeadv.ac.in) identifies Class 11 content in 40% of Advanced questions.</p>
